{
  "id": "odd-even-linked-list",
  "questionId": "odd-even-linked-list",
  "slug": "odd-even-linked-list",
  "title": "Odd Even Linked List",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Fast slow pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "fast & slow pointers",
    "linked list",
    "two pointers"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "you're essentially splitting one list into two interleaved lists based on position parity - can you build both in a single pass without extra storage?",
    "keep two pointers, one sitting at the current odd-position node and one at the current even-position node, and advance each by skipping over the other's node",
    "at each step, link the odd pointer's node to the node two ahead (skipping the even node in between), then do the same for the even pointer, alternating forward",
    "once you reach the end, attach the head of the even list to the tail of the odd list to join them together"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "losing track of the even list's head once you start rewriting next pointers, making it impossible to attach it at the end",
    "off-by-one errors on odd vs even positions when the list length is small (1 or 2 nodes)",
    "not handling the empty list input"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A singly linked list is given to you as an array `nums`, where `nums[i]` is the value of the i-th node in order.\n\n**Using 1-indexed positions, group together all nodes at odd positions followed by all nodes at even positions, preserving the relative order within each group, and return the resulting list's values in order.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= nums.length <= 10^4",
      "positions are 1-indexed"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "an empty list",
      "a single-node list",
      "a two-node list"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]",
        "output": "[1, 3, 5, 2, 4]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 3, 5, 2, 4]",
        "explanation": "odd positions (1,3,5) are values 1,3,5; even positions (2,4) are values 2,4"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [2, 1, 3, 5, 6, 4, 7]",
        "output": "[2, 3, 6, 7, 1, 5, 4]",
        "expectedOutput": "[2, 3, 6, 7, 1, 5, 4]",
        "explanation": "odd positions (1,3,5,7) give 2,3,6,7; even positions (2,4,6) give 1,5,4"
